{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162790" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-162790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162790" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ given that $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x=2\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}4x=\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n4x=-(x-1)\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $16$ | $-\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162801" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-162801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{33}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162801" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-1=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-99240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "5-3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "5-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5-3\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=8x-3y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 35.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8=4\\lambda x \\\\ -3=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-4y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ 32{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-4y=∓4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4,1),\\,(4,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(4,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-35$ | $35$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4,-1),$ and its maximum value is $35.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-99274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "35"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{35}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "35"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2(y-1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y=-\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162802" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-162802" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162802" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{20}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162802" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}\\le 25.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}<25$ is $(0,0).$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=25.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=25\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-16y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,10y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -16y=10\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ -2y(8+5\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 5,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(5,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 5,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-40$ | $25$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ and its minimum value is $-40.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-162780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16"] ["math" "-20"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-40"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-40"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y\\le 6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y=6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x-2,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\lambda }{\\lambda -1} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\lambda }{\\lambda -1}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(3,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(3,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $6$ | $22$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-162799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "63"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}$ given that $2{x}^{2}+y\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+y=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ y=\\frac{\\lambda }{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{5}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-162782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+10{y}^{2}$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4$ is $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x & =2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y & =2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-99255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "40"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "40"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-163055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-6$ given that $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}\\le 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-163055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=5.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨\\frac{x}{2},-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x=\\frac{\\lambda x}{2} \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- If $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, then assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =5\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).$\n\n- If $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, then from the first equation we have $x=0$, and assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not possible.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $-6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-163055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-163055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-163055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "24"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-163055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the minimum possible sum of three positive numbers whose product is $27.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the three numbers. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y+z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda yz \\\\ 1=\\lambda xz \\\\ 1=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $1=0.$ Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz & =xz \\\\ yz-xz & =0 \\\\ (y-x)z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =27 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =27 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=3+3+3=9.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9,3,1) & =9+3+1 \\\\  & =13 \\\\  & >f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-162788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "29"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-162788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-37701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the product of sines of angle measures of a triangle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-37701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0<z<\\pi ,$ we have that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\ne 0.$ On the other hand, if $y-x=\\pi \\cdot n,$ with $n$ an integer, we obtain that $x>\\pi $ or $y>\\pi ,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-37701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-37701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-37701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-37701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99163" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-99163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "42\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "48\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "54\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "60\\,\\text{in}"] ["math" "72\\,\\text{in}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99163" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60\\,\\text{in}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $108.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}yz=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}xyz=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-99204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "105\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "125"] ["math" "216"] ["math" "256"] ["math" "25\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-99204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "216"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-98104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangular box without a lid has a surface area of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum possible volume of the box." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-98104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $x,y$, and $z$ be the box's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n2xz+2yz+xy=48.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=2xz+2yz+xy.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2z+y,2z+x,2x+2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}yz=\\lambda (2z+y) \\\\ xz=\\lambda (2z+x) \\\\ xy=\\lambda (2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}xyz=\\lambda x(2z+y) \\\\ xyz=\\lambda y(2z+x) \\\\ xyz=\\lambda z(2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $xyz=0,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2z+y) & =y(2z+x) \\\\ 2xz+xy & =2yz+xy \\\\ xz & =yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ne 0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz+2yz+xy & =48 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ 12{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(4,4,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(4,4,2)=4\\cdot 4\\cdot 2=32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,5.5) & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5.5 \\\\  & =22 \\\\  & <f(4,4,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-98104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44cebed411842b3f5a6b709854a13cc1597e7d64d857b09eff22d61c05f20462"} {"question" "q-98104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-98104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "24{\\text{ cm}}^{3}"] ["math" "32{\\text{ cm}}^{3}"] ["math" "36{\\text{ cm}}^{3}"] ["math" "48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}"] ["math" "54{\\text{ cm}}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "q-98104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32{\\text{ cm}}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/state.json" "file_sha256" "f8a8aee5b22fc91add0706c3aaee45f631f27de58c2458f012aeef3c8c86eee7"} {"question" "e-12457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/example-12457.json" "file_sha256" "920b095b8116a4dcaba3718828f9def652a26c3b9c73cc20dd1df9d43e2491d3"} {"question" "e-12457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2  = 1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 1=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  = 1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad  \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $\\dfrac23.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/example-12457.json" "file_sha256" "920b095b8116a4dcaba3718828f9def652a26c3b9c73cc20dd1df9d43e2491d3"} {"question" "e-8690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = 3x^2 - y^2$ given that $3x^2 + 2y^2\\leq 16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/example-8690.json" "file_sha256" "5f113f255a67b439464f9b7b72375c34cf53df34a2bae0be5fdad9cf20c937a5"} {"question" "e-8690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=\\left(0,  0 \\right).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 3x^2 + 2y^2  = 16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6x=6\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right).$\n\n- Note that assuming $\\lambda = 1$ from the first equation leads to $y=0,$ which we already have a solution for, and $\\lambda = -\\dfrac12$ from the second equation leads to $x=0,$ which we also have a solution for. So, we have found all the candidates on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left( 0,0 \\right), \\qquad  \\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0\\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left( 0,0 \\right)$ | $\\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$ | $\\left( \\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2  \\right),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/example-8690.json" "file_sha256" "5f113f255a67b439464f9b7b72375c34cf53df34a2bae0be5fdad9cf20c937a5"} {"question" "e-8691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of cosines of the angle measures of a triangle, given that all three angles are acute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/example-8691.json" "file_sha256" "514abcc314b1d179a7f00263f7ec01f7052a77324bcea69375b39171f20c0e3a"} {"question" "e-8691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075643/example-8691.json" "file_sha256" "514abcc314b1d179a7f00263f7ec01f7052a77324bcea69375b39171f20c0e3a"}] "usage" {"q-37701" [] "e-8691" [] "q-98104" [] "e-8690" [] "e-12457" []} "basis" 2758 "retractions" [[17592186198945 :question/problem "A rectangular box without a lid has a surface area of $48cm^{2}$. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum possible volume of the box."] [17592186197975 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1$."] [17592186197975 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ \\\\\n⟨2x, 1⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨2x, 1⟩ &= λ⟨4x, 6y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{2x = 4λx; 1 = 6λy⇒{x(1 - 2λ) = 0; λy = \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y) = 1$ implies\n$2(0)^{2} + 3y^{2} = 1⇒y = ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right)$.\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives\n$\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}$.\nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y) =1$ implies\n$2x^{2} + 3(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} = 1⇒x = ± \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at\n\n$$\n\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)\n$$\n\nand its maximum value is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac23\n$$"] [17592186198167 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = 3x^2 - y^2$ given that $3x^2 + 2y^2\\leq 16$."] [17592186198167 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ \\\\\n⟨6x,-2y⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution\n\n$$\n(x,y)=\\left(0, 0 \\right)\n$$\n\nThis point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint\n\n$$\ng(x,y)= 3x^2 + 2y^2 = 16\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= λ∇g(x, y,) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y), f_{y}(x, y)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y), g_{y}(x, y)⟩ \\\\\n⟨6x,-2y⟩ &= λ⟨6x, 4y⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{6x = 6λx; - 2y = 4λy⇒{x(1 - λ) = 0; y(2λ + 1) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y) =16$ implies\n$2y^{2}|= 16⇒y = ± 2\\sqrt{2}$,\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$.\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y) =16$ implies\n$3x^{2}|= 16⇒x = ± \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}$,\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$.\n- Note that assuming $\\lambda = 1$ from the first equation leads to $y=0$, which we already have a solution for, and $\\lambda = -\\dfrac12$ from the second equation leads to $x=0$, which we also have a solution for. So, we have found all the candidates on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0 \\right), \\qquad \\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0,0 \\right)$ | $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)\n$$\n\nand its minimum value is $-8$."] [17592186198168 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$, $y$, and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z}\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z) = x+y+z\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= λ∇g(x, y, z) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}(x, y, z), f_{y}(x, y, z), f_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}(x, y, z), g_{y}(x, y, z), g_{z}(x, y, z)⟩ \\\\\n⟨ - \\sin x,-\\sin y,-\\sin z⟩ &= λ⟨1, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{-\\sin x = λ; - \\sin y = λ; - \\sin z = λ\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x &= \\sin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that $x = y$.\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x$. So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= π \\\\\nx + x + x &= π \\\\\n3x &= π \\\\\nx &= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nand the maximum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) + \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) + \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{5π}{12}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{3}) + \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\cos (\\frac{5π}{12}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1.47 \\\\\n&< f(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186197468 17592186197469 17592186197470 17592186197471 17592186197472 17592186197906 17592186197907 17592186197908 17592186197909 17592186197910] "immutable_fields" [[17592186197467 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197467 :answer-field/id] [17592186197467 :answer-field/key] [17592186197467 :answer-field/type] [17592186197905 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197905 :answer-field/id] [17592186197905 :answer-field/key] [17592186197905 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
